  "Question": "The pair of equations \\(3^{x+y}=81\\) and \\(81^{x-y}=3\\) has:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "no common solution",
    "B": "the solution \\(x=2,\\) \\(y=2\\)",
    "C": "the solution \\(x=2\\dfrac12,\\) \\(y=1\\dfrac12\\)",
    "D": "a common solution in positive and negative integers",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Both 81 and 3 are powers of 3, so rewriting with that base lets us compare exponents. Since powers of 3 strictly increase as their exponents increase,\n\\[\n3^{x+y}=3^4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x+y=4.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n81^{x-y}=3^{4(x-y)}=3^1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x-y=\\frac14.\n\\]\nAdding these two linear equations eliminates \\(y\\); subtracting them eliminates \\(x\\). Thus\n\\[\n2x=\\frac{17}{4},\\qquad 2y=\\frac{15}{4},\n\\]\nso \\(x=17/8\\) and \\(y=15/8\\). Their sum is 4 and their difference is \\(1/4\\), so they satisfy both original equations. Every transformation was equivalent, making this the unique common solution. It is neither of the pairs in B or C, and it is not an integer pair. Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
